"""Microsoft's fonts and Visual C++ runtimes, which many Windows programs
expect, fetched in the background after boot so that programs simply
find them. Raye does not ship either: both come from Microsoft.

    raye-windows-components status                    JSON, any user
    raye-windows-components install [--quiet] [NAME...]   root
    raye-windows-components remove [--quiet] NAME...      root

NAME is "fonts" or "vcredist"; install with no name adds both. The
timer runs it as root; Settings can run it through pkexec. install exits 0 when everything is in place; when something
could not be added (no network, a file that failed its check) it exits
1, or 0 with --quiet, which also prints nothing on stdout. status says
what is still missing, so it can be offered again later.

fonts:    Microsoft's core fonts (Arial, Times New Roman, Verdana...),
          the same eleven files the corefonts project has kept since
          Microsoft gave them away, each pinned by its SHA-256 and so is
          every font inside, put in /usr/local/share/fonts for every
          account. Programs see the system's fonts, so every Windows
          space gets them at once. (The archive's installer package
          cannot be used: it depends on the release upgrader and the
          subscription client, which Raye keeps out.)
vcredist: vc_redist.x64.exe and vc_redist.x86.exe from Microsoft's
          permanent links, kept in /var/lib/raye/windows/redist/ for
          raye-windows-prepare to install into each space. A file is
          kept only when its Authenticode signature checks out with
          osslsigncode against Microsoft's root certificates, which are
          pinned by their SHA-256 in redist-signers.txt; Microsoft
          republishing the file or changing its signing certificate
          does not break this, a file signed by anyone else is refused.
          With them, the 2005 to 2013 runtimes, which Microsoft no
          longer changes: those are kept only as the exact files pinned
          in OLDER below.

raye-windows-components.timer runs "install --quiet" in the background a
little after boot, so programs find all of this without being asked.
"""
